# Shell scripts must keep LF endings whatever the contributor's platform does
# with the rest of the tree. A CRLF docker-entrypoint.sh does not fail visibly:
# the kernel reads "/bin/sh\r" as the interpreter and the container dies with
# "no such file or directory", naming a file that is plainly there.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by Linux tooling, not by an editor on Windows.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Everything else is text with the platform ending on checkout and LF in the
# repository. Stated rather than left to autocrlf: seven of the integration
# modules were stored with a bare trailing CR for a year, which git reads as
# binary, and no per-file diff or blame worked on them while it did.
* text=auto

# This fixture is the byte-for-byte published v1.6.1 manifest, not editable JSON.
tests/fixtures/post-release-native-v1.6.1/qualification-manifest.json -text
tests/fixtures/post-release-native-v1.6.1/baseline-qualification-summary.json -text

# Retained KVM receipts are also byte-hashed evidence; CRLF conversion invalidates them.
tests/fixtures/linux-kvm-privileged-capability-evidence/result.json -text
tests/fixtures/linux-kvm-privileged-capability-evidence/privileged-result.json -text

# The guest runtime bundle is approved by hashing each file's bytes, so those bytes have
# to be the same on every machine - text=auto is not enough for that. It reports "auto"
# for a file holding a bare CR while quietly storing that file verbatim, and two versions
# differing by one CR then both check out unchanged under one approved hash. Forcing the
# ending here means Git stores exactly one form, and any drift shows up as a failed hash
# rather than as two files Git considers equally correct.
# windowsBaselineGuestRuntimeBundleClosure.test.js asserts this list against the bundle's.
scripts/qualification/windows-baseline-guest-executor.mjs text eol=lf
scripts/qualification/windows-baseline-guest-runner.mjs text eol=lf
